def lab(label,text):
    p=D.add_paragraph(); r=p.add_run(label+' '); r.bold=True; runs(p,cit(text)); p.paragraph_format.space_after=Pt(6)
lab('Background.',"Gastroschisis affects about 3 per 10 000 births and its prevalence is rising. Surgeons choose fascial closure (FC), delayed closure after a silo (DC) or sutureless closure (SL) according to bowel condition, so raw comparisons are confounded. No review has pooled all three strategies with prediction intervals.")
lab('Methods.',"We followed PRISMA 2020 (PROSPERO CRD420261519114). We searched five databases, ClinicalTrials.gov and ten journals on 22 November 2026 for trials and comparative studies of neonates. Pairwise random-effects meta-analysis used REML, Hartung-Knapp intervals and prediction intervals. Risk of bias used ROBINS-I and RoB 2, and certainty used GRADE. Studies without extractable arm-level data were synthesised narratively. Data and code are archived at Zenodo «[DOI to be inserted]».")
lab('Results.',f"Of 9,158 database and register records, 42 studies were included, 35 with arm-level data on about 5,800 infants (three randomized trials) and 7 in a narrative synthesis. Compared with FC, DC was associated with a longer stay ({dd(dl)}, {dl['k']} studies, prediction interval {pint(dl)}), more ventilator days ({dd(dv)}) and more parenteral nutrition ({dd(dp)}). Time to full feeds, sepsis ({rr(ds)}), necrotizing enterocolitis and mortality did not differ. SL did not change stay ({dd(sl)}) and was associated with fewer ventilator days ({dd(sv)}), with a wide interval for hernia ({rr(sh)}). Certainty was low or very low.")
lab('Conclusions.',"Delayed closure after a silo goes with longer stay, ventilation and parenteral nutrition than fascial closure, but confounding by indication limits any causal reading, so a silo should be reserved for infants who cannot tolerate primary reduction. Sutureless closure is a reasonable option in simple gastroschisis, with shorter ventilation and equal stay, although hernia risk is unresolved. Trials stratified by bowel condition are needed.")
D.add_page_break()
# =================== INTRODUCTION ===================
H('Introduction')
para("Gastroschisis is a congenital defect of the anterior abdominal wall. Its prevalence is about 3 per 10 000 births, it is higher in young mothers, and it varies by region [@feldkamp]. Birth prevalence in the United States has risen over three decades [@schuh]. Timing of surgery and choice of closure technique shape the time an infant spends on the ventilator, the time to full enteral feeds, the risk of sepsis and the length of stay [@bhat]. Three strategies are in common use. Fascial closure (FC) repairs the fascia with sutures at the first operation. Delayed closure (DC) reduces the bowel in stages in a preformed or surgical silo and closes the defect later. Sutureless closure (SL) leaves the fascial defect to close around the umbilical cord or a dressing [@petrosyan].")
para("Earlier systematic reviews compared pairs of strategies. Reviews of FC against silo-based repair found similar survival and conflicting results for feeding, stay and ventilation [@kunz; @allin]. Reviews of flap or sutureless closure found short-term outcomes that were comparable to or better than sutured repair, with more umbilical hernias [@youssef; @miyake; @diyaolu; @rao]. These reviews share a weakness. Infants with edematous or damaged bowel are more likely to receive a silo, and they stay longer in hospital for that reason. A raw comparison of strategies therefore mixes the effect of the technique with the effect of the bowel. Most trials are small, and registries cannot separate the two without adjustment.")
para("We did not find a review that pooled all three strategies against a common comparator with a model that allows for between-study heterogeneity and reports prediction intervals. No review separated studies restricted to simple gastroschisis from studies that included complex cases, and none tested whether the evidence allows an indirect comparison of DC with SL. We aimed to estimate the association of DC and SL with in-hospital outcomes relative to FC, to examine how far design, case mix and data conversion change those estimates, and to rate the confidence the evidence deserves.")
# =================== METHODS ===================
H('Methods')
para("This review follows PRISMA 2020 [@prisma]. «[The protocol was registered in PROSPERO as CRD420261519114. The record must be amended to describe the pairwise design, the complexity strata and the new search, and the amendment date should be stated here.]» We wrote the analysis plan before pooling any data, and it appears in the Supplementary Material. We included randomized trials and comparative cohort or case-control studies of neonates with gastroschisis that compared at least two of three strategies and reported outcomes for each arm or an adjusted effect with its standard error. FC was sutured fascial closure at the first operation. DC was placement of a preformed or surgical silo followed by closure at a later operation. SL was closure without fascial sutures, using the umbilical cord or a dressing. We analysed flap and plastic closure apart from SL. We excluded single-arm series, case reports, reviews, studies of omphalocele alone, and studies that compared timing of closure without comparing techniques. The primary outcome was length of hospital stay. Secondary outcomes were ventilator days, time to full enteral feeds, days of parenteral nutrition, sepsis or bloodstream infection, necrotizing enterocolitis, mortality, and ventral or umbilical hernia.")
para("We searched PubMed, Scopus, the Cochrane Library, EBSCOhost (MEDLINE with Full Text) and Europe PMC (which includes medRxiv) on 22 November 2026. We also searched ClinicalTrials.gov. To reach reports that the databases do not index, we searched eight Indonesian journals listed in Sinta and two international surgery journals through Crossref metadata (Paediatrica Indonesiana, Sari Pediatri, Majalah Obstetri dan Ginekologi, Intisari Sains Medis, Medicina, Jurnal e-CliniC, Medical Scope Journal and Jurnal Ilmu Kedokteran, plus the Journal of Neonatal Surgery and the Journal of Pediatric Surgery Case Reports). The strategy combined a gastroschisis block with a closure-technique block and set no limit on language, date or design. In PubMed and Europe PMC it retrieved all 64 records assessed in an earlier version of this review, and in Europe PMC it retrieved all seven independent reviews and trials that we used as a recall check. Supplementary Table S1 and the Boolean test report give the full strings and counts. We read Spanish-language reports in the original.")
para("«[Two reviewers screened titles, abstracts and full texts independently and settled disagreements with a third reviewer. Agreement was kappa = ___.]» We obtained the full text of all 42 eligible reports. For each study we extracted design, setting, population, arm sizes and outcome data. We recorded continuous outcomes as means with standard deviations or as medians with ranges or interquartile ranges, and binary outcomes as event counts. When a study reported simple and complex gastroschisis separately, we recorded both strata. When silo groups were defined by time to closure, we combined them into one DC arm with the standard formula for combining groups. The extraction workbook in the Supplementary Material lists every value with its source table and conversion. «[A second reviewer checked each extracted value against the full text.]» We did not contact study authors for missing data in this version of the review.")
para("We converted medians with ranges or interquartile ranges to means and standard deviations with the Box-Cox method of McGrath and colleagues [@mcgrath], which allows for skew and served as the primary approach. The formulas of Wan and colleagues [@wan] served as a sensitivity analysis. We converted standard errors to standard deviations and fixed the random seed at 20261123 so that the analysis can be reproduced. We did not pool a study for an outcome when it reported only medians without any measure of spread, or means without standard deviations.")
para("We rated non-randomized studies with ROBINS-I [@robinsi] and trials with RoB 2 [@rob2]. «[Two reviewers rated each study independently and resolved differences by discussion.]» We rated certainty of evidence with GRADE. Bodies of evidence from non-randomized studies started at high certainty within the ROBINS-I framework and were rated down for risk of bias, inconsistency, indirectness, imprecision and publication bias [@grade; @grade_robinsi]. We set minimally important differences at 3 days for stay, feeds and parenteral nutrition, at 1 day for ventilation, and at a risk ratio of 0.75 to 1.25 for binary outcomes.")
para("We chose the statistical model from the structure of the data. The comparisons are DC against FC and SL against FC, and most studies contribute one effect per outcome, so a conventional pairwise random-effects meta-analysis was the primary model [@balduzzi]. We estimated between-study variance with restricted maximum likelihood and used the Hartung-Knapp adjustment for the confidence interval, because most analyses included fewer than 20 studies [@hk]. We report 95% prediction intervals [@inthout]. We pooled continuous outcomes as mean differences in days and binary outcomes as risk ratios with inverse-variance weights and a continuity correction of 0.5. Each study entered once per outcome. When a study reported a simple stratum and an all-infant stratum, the primary analysis used the all-infant stratum and a sensitivity analysis used the simple stratum.")
para("We summarised heterogeneity with I² and τ² and explored it with subgroup analyses by design (randomized or not) and by population (restricted to simple gastroschisis or not), with a meta-regression on publication year, and with leave-one-out analysis. We drew funnel plots and ran the Egger test [@egger] only for comparisons with ten or more studies. Sensitivity analyses used the Wan conversion, simple-stratum data only, trials only, non-randomized studies only, exclusion of studies with approximated arm sizes, exclusion of every converted median, and inclusion of flap or plastic closure in SL.")
para("Eligible studies that gave no usable arm-level data, for example because they reported only adjusted associations, medians without spread or a before-and-after comparison, were not pooled. We synthesised them narratively, grouped by comparison, and recorded the design, the direction of effect, the adjustment and the reason they could not be pooled. For the pooled studies we also drew harvest plots that count studies by the direction of effect for each outcome, with bar height for risk of bias and sample-size class, so that the direction of the evidence can be read without the model.")
para("Three secondary analyses addressed dependence between effects. Two three-arm studies contribute a shared arm to both comparisons and supply the only direct data on DC against SL. We therefore fitted a frequentist network meta-analysis with netmeta [@netmeta]. Every network is connected through FC, and only those two studies close a loop. We examined direct and indirect estimates by node splitting and read the P-scores with caution [@pscore]. We also fitted a multilevel model with study and effect as random levels, the contrast as a moderator and cluster-robust standard errors [@metafor]. Finally, we analysed separately the contrast between sutureless and sutured closure after silo placement. We ran all analyses in R 4.6.2 with the meta, metafor, netmeta and estmeansd packages, and the scripts and data are in the Supplementary Material.")
para("«[Deviations from the registered protocol. The registered protocol planned a network meta-analysis of the three strategies. The full-text review showed that the network is a star around FC with a loop closed only by two three-arm studies, so pairwise meta-analysis became the primary model and the network analysis a secondary check. The search was rebuilt with a single search date, wider closure terms and a recall check. The complexity strata were fixed before pooling, and flap closure was separated from sutureless closure. The sensitivity analyses that exclude Hawkins from the Wan conversion and exclude studies with approximated arm sizes were added after we saw the extracted data. The authors should compare this paragraph with the registration and edit it.]»")
# =================== RESULTS ===================
H('Results')
para("The database searches retrieved 9,108 records (PubMed 1,912, Scopus 3,375, Cochrane Library 92, EBSCOhost MEDLINE with Full Text 1,788 and Europe PMC 1,941), and ClinicalTrials.gov added 50 registered studies. After removal of 3,146 duplicates, 6,012 records were screened and 138 reports were assessed in full. Hand-searching of ten journals identified 38 records, four of which the database searches had already found, and 9 reports were assessed in full and none was included. In total 42 studies were included (Figure 1). Of these, 35 gave arm-level data (Table 1). Twenty-nine entered a primary analysis. Three entered only sensitivity analyses because they studied flap or plastic closure or gave counts derived from percentages [@emami2015; @orion2011; @anand2025], and three were described but not pooled because their comparator or contrast did not fit the primary comparisons [@allotey2007; @charlesworth2014; @dariel2015]. Seven reports gave no usable arm-level data and were synthesised narratively (Table 4) [@weinsheimer2008; @gonzalez2018; @williamson2022; @wong2020; @manson2012; @joharifard2022; @dore2016].")
para("The 35 studies were published from 2000 to 2025 and came from North America (n = 23), Europe (n = 6), Latin America (n = 4) and Asia (n = 2). Three were randomized trials [@pastor2008; @bruzoni2017; @poola2019], and one of them stopped early. The others were single-centre or multicentre cohorts and case-control studies, including eight registry, consortium or multicentre cohorts [@murthy2014; @gurien2017; @owen2010; @stanger2014; @emami2015; @hawkins2020; @fraser2020; @fraser2022]. Twelve studies restricted inclusion to simple gastroschisis or reported a simple stratum, and the others included complex cases without separate data. Twenty-two studies compared DC with FC and twelve compared SL or flap closure with FC. Two studies had three arms [@mcnamara2011; @avila2025]. Two compared a mixed comparator (primary closure plus surgical silo) with a preformed silo and could not enter the FC comparisons [@allotey2007; @charlesworth2014]. Two studies compared sutureless with sutured closure after silo placement [@fraser2020; @dariel2015].")
