para("Overall risk of bias was serious in 31 of the 32 non-randomized studies and moderate in one matched case-control study (Figure 5). Confounding was serious in every study except the matched one, because surgeons chose the technique according to bowel condition and in several cohorts the technique changed with era. Missing outcome data raised concern in two registries. Stay was available for 76% of infants in one and 85% in the other, and time to full feeds was available for only 26% in the first [@hawkins2020; @gurien2017]. One trial was at low risk of bias [@pastor2008], and two had some concerns because they were small and unblinded, and one stopped early [@bruzoni2017; @poola2019].")
a=S('Non-randomised','LOS','DC_vs_FC'); r_=S('Randomised','LOS','DC_vs_FC'); s_=S('Simple','LOS','DC_vs_FC'); w_=S('Wan','LOS','DC_vs_FC'); x_=S('Excluding converted','LOS','DC_vs_FC'); ap=S('Excluding approximated','LOS','DC_vs_FC')
para(f"Nineteen studies with 2,460 infants contributed to length of stay. DC was associated with {f1(dl['est'])} more days in hospital (95% CI {f1(dl['lo'])} to {f1(dl['hi'])}, I² {i2(dl)}, Figure 2). The 95% prediction interval of {pint(dl)} days includes both benefit and harm, so the effect in a new setting could be null. Ventilator days were higher after DC ({dd(dv)}, 16 studies, I² {i2(dv)}), and so was parenteral nutrition ({dd(dp)}, 11 studies, I² {i2(dp)}). Time to full enteral feeds did not differ ({dd(df)}, 10 studies). Sepsis ({rr(ds)}, 10 studies), necrotizing enterocolitis ({rr(dn)}, 7 studies) and mortality ({rr(dm)}, 9 studies) did not differ. Ventral hernia was less frequent after DC, but three studies informed it and the interval included no difference ({rr(dh)}).")
para(f"The result for stay held in the leave-one-out analysis (pooled difference 5.8 to 7.6 days, P from 0.002 to 0.025) and after exclusion of studies with approximated arm sizes ({dd(ap)}). It lost statistical significance when we kept only studies that reported means and standard deviations ({dd(x_)}, 11 studies) and with the Wan conversion ({dd(w_)}, 18 studies). In studies restricted to simple gastroschisis the estimate was similar and imprecise ({dd(s_)}, 6 studies). The two trials with extractable data on stay (n = 92) gave {dd(r_)}, an interval too wide to inform the question [@pastor2008; @poola2019]. Subgroup tests did not show a difference by population (P = 0.66) or design (P = 0.11), and Table 3 sets the main sensitivity analyses side by side. A meta-regression on publication year was not significant (0.4 days per year, P = 0.25), and the funnel plot showed no clear asymmetry (Egger P = 0.56). The multilevel model gave a similar estimate (7.3 days, 95% CI 3.4 to 11.3).")
_s=len(D.element.body)-1
fig(os.path.join(AN,'forest','forest_LOS_DC_vs_FC.png'),'Figure 2. Length of hospital stay after delayed silo-based closure (DC) compared with fascial closure (FC). Random-effects meta-analysis with Hartung-Knapp confidence interval. The red bar shows the 95% prediction interval. Positive differences mean a longer stay after DC.')
BLK['F2']=(_s,len(D.element.body)-1)
para('Vote counting by direction of effect agreed with the pooled estimates (Figure 6). For length of stay, 15 of 19 studies showed a longer stay after DC and four a shorter stay. The same pattern held for ventilator days (10 longer and 6 shorter of 16 studies), parenteral nutrition (10 longer and 1 shorter of 11) and time to full feeds (6 longer and 4 shorter of 10). Studies at lower risk of bias, which are the trials and the matched case-control study, did not point in a single direction.')
para(f"Eight studies with 416 infants informed length of stay, and SL did not change it ({dd(sl)}, I² {i2(sl)}, Figure 3). Ventilator days were lower after SL ({dd(sv)}, 5 studies, I² {i2(sv)}). The direction was the same with the Wan conversion ({dd(S('Wan','VD','SL_vs_FC'))}), in non-randomized studies only ({dd(S('Non-randomised','VD','SL_vs_FC'))}) and when flap and plastic closure were added ({dd(S('Including','VD','SL_vs_FC'))}, 6 studies). The difference was not significant when we kept the three studies that reported means and standard deviations ({dd(S('Excluding converted','VD','SL_vs_FC'))}). Time to full feeds ({dd(sf)}, 6 studies) and parenteral nutrition ({dd(sp_)}, 4 studies) did not differ, and sepsis showed no clear difference ({rr(ss)}, 5 studies). Two studies reported hernia, and SL had more events with a very wide interval ({rr(sh)}). One study with events reported mortality. In the multilevel model SL was associated with 2.4 fewer ventilator days (95% CI 0.6 to 4.2).")
_s=len(D.element.body)-1
