fig(os.path.join(AN,'forest','forest_VD_SL_vs_FC.png'),'Figure 4. Ventilator days after sutureless closure (SL) compared with fascial closure (FC). Negative differences mean fewer ventilator days after SL.')
BLK['F4']=(_s,len(D.element.body)-1)
para('Vote counting for SL showed the same direction (Figure 7). Five of five studies reported fewer ventilator days, five of eight reported a shorter stay, three of four reported less parenteral nutrition and three of six reported earlier full feeds.')
para("Two studies compared sutureless with sutured closure in infants who already had a silo. In the Midwest Pediatric Surgery Consortium, which studied uncomplicated gastroschisis, 30 infants had sutureless and 212 had sutured closure. Median stay was 36 days against 33.5 days, ventilator time was 0.5 days against 4 days, and surgical site infection occurred in 1 of 30 against 29 of 212 [@fraser2020]. In a Canadian cohort of 23 plastic and 41 surgical secondary closures, ventilator time, sepsis (12 of 23 and 13 of 41) and umbilical hernia (5 of 23 and 6 of 41) were similar [@dariel2015]. Two studies are too few to pool.")
para("The network for stay contained 25 studies from three designs, namely DC against FC, SL against FC, and the two three-arm studies. Estimates against FC agreed with the pairwise results at 7.1 days (95% CI 3.4 to 10.8) for DC and 0.5 days (95% CI −5.2 to 6.2) for SL. For ventilator days DC added 2.7 days (95% CI 1.1 to 4.2) and SL removed 2.5 days (95% CI −5.1 to 0.1). For parenteral nutrition DC added 7.3 days (95% CI 3.5 to 11.0) and SL changed nothing. Direct and indirect estimates for DC against SL did not disagree for stay (P = 0.19), ventilator days (P = 0.76) or parenteral nutrition (P = 0.95). The loop rests on two small studies, so these tests have little power, and we did not interpret the P-scores. Supplementary Figure S1 shows the network estimates.")
para("Certainty was very low for stay, ventilator days, feeds, parenteral nutrition and hernia after DC, and for stay, feeds, parenteral nutrition, sepsis and hernia after SL. It was low for sepsis, necrotizing enterocolitis and mortality after DC and for ventilator days after SL (Table 2). We rated risk of bias down by one level in every case and did not rate down for publication bias.")
