para("Seven reports could not be pooled (Table 4). Four of them analysed delayed or silo-based closure against earlier or primary closure and agreed in direction with the pooled estimates. In 4,459 neonates from the Pediatric Health Information System, delayed closure after the first day went with longer stay and more days of parenteral nutrition, also after excluding complicated cases, and hospitals differed widely in both outcomes [@gonzalez2018]. In a single centre with 80 infants, delayed closure went with a 57% higher index-admission cost and with longer stay, parenteral nutrition, ventilation and time to goal feeds after adjustment [@wong2020]. A cohort of 27 infants found fewer ventilator days (median 4 against 13) and fewer parenteral nutrition days (12 against 20) after primary closure than after a surgical silo, with similar stay [@dore2016]. Two reports concerned sutureless closure. In the Canadian Pediatric Surgery Network, sutureless closure delayed the start of feeds but did not lengthen parenteral nutrition or stay after adjustment, and failure to achieve primary closure was the only technique-related factor linked to worse outcomes [@weinsheimer2008]. After a standardised protocol raised sutureless bedside closure from 32.5% to 71.0%, median ventilation fell from 4 to 2 days without more complications [@joharifard2022]. The remaining two reports gave no contrast between strategies. A national readmissions database showed wide variation in coding of sutureless and fascial repair across 218 hospitals [@williamson2022], and a five-centre comparison of African and Western units described regional differences in care [@manson2012]. The narrative evidence therefore points the same way as the pooled data and adds no new reason to doubt it. All seven reports share the confounding that limits the pooled studies.")
# =================== DISCUSSION ===================
H('Discussion')
para(f"In 35 comparative studies, delayed closure after a silo was associated with {f1(dl['est'])} more days in hospital, {f1(dv['est'])} more ventilator days and {f1(dp['est'])} more days of parenteral nutrition than fascial closure. Sepsis, necrotizing enterocolitis and mortality did not differ. Sutureless closure left hospital stay unchanged and was associated with {f1(-float(sv['est']))} fewer ventilator days. Its hernia estimate was too imprecise to interpret. The direction of the ventilator result for SL matches a recent meta-analysis of 23 studies [@rao]. The network analysis shows that the data do not contradict an indirect comparison of DC with SL, and it adds little else.")
para("Surgeons place a silo when the bowel is edematous, when the abdomen will not accept reduction, or when the infant is unstable, and these infants stay longer with any technique. Selection of this kind could produce differences of the size we found. Four observations fit that reading. The two trials, in which allocation did not depend on bowel condition, showed no difference in stay, although with 92 infants they cannot exclude one. The estimate lost significance when we kept only studies that reported means and standard deviations, when we used the Wan conversion and when we restricted the analysis to simple gastroschisis. Between-study heterogeneity was large, and the prediction interval included no difference. The difference in ventilator days ranged from 2 days fewer after DC in a trial to 12 days more in a cohort [@pastor2008; @lobo2010]. The data support an association between DC and longer care in routine practice. They cannot show that DC causes it.")
para("The three trials give the only comparisons free of selection by bowel condition. In the multicentre trial of a spring-loaded silo, 54 infants had a mean stay of 49.1 days with a silo and 43.2 days with primary closure, and mean ventilator days were 3.2 and 5.3 [@pastor2008]. A small trial of bedside silo against primary closure (n = 38) found median stays of 26.5 and 24 days [@poola2019]. The trial of sutureless against sutured closure (n = 39) found a longer stay after sutureless closure, 49.3 days against 31.4 days [@bruzoni2017]. All three are small, and none shows a shorter stay with the silo or the sutureless approach.")
para("Fewer ventilator days after sutureless closure is a plausible effect of the technique, since it avoids general anaesthesia and closure under tension. The direction held across conversion methods, study designs and the addition of flap closure. The estimate rests on five studies and 255 infants, though, and it was not significant when we kept only studies that reported means and standard deviations. Sutureless closure did not shorten hospital stay, and the one trial found a longer stay [@bruzoni2017]. The hernia signal agrees with earlier work [@rao; @diyaolu]. Two studies cannot establish its size, and in the largest consortium cohort 39% of peri-umbilical hernias had closed spontaneously, and 46% in the sutureless group [@fraser2022].")
para("The data shaped the choice of model. Almost every study contributes one effect per outcome, and the networks are stars except for two three-arm studies. A network meta-analysis would add little to two pairwise comparisons, so we made the pairwise random-effects model primary and treated the network and the multilevel model as checks. Both agreed with it. Conversion of medians also matters. Many studies report medians with ranges, and a range that stretches to hundreds of days produces an implausible standard deviation. We used the Box-Cox method as the primary approach and dropped the one study with a parenteral nutrition range up to 1718 days from the Wan sensitivity analysis. We report the results of both methods. Two large registries gave no group sizes for continuous outcomes, so we approximated them from the total with data. Excluding these studies left the result for stay unchanged.")
para("This review searched five databases, one register and eight Indonesian and two surgery journals with a tested Boolean string, read all 42 eligible reports in full, separated simple from complex gastroschisis in advance, and reported prediction intervals and a sensitivity analysis for every conversion. Its limits come mostly from the evidence. Almost all studies are observational and unadjusted, so confounding by indication is the leading threat. Registries overlap in time and may share centres, and we could not check patient overlap. Ventilator days and time to full feeds are defined differently across studies, and several reports give medians only. Only three trials exist, and one stopped early. Sepsis definitions differ and deaths are few. We did not contact authors for missing data, which could have rescued some studies that reported medians without spread or means without standard deviations. Hand-searching covered ten journals, so reports from other regional journals may be missing, and four hand-searched records still lack full text. Embase was not searched. We could not analyse complex gastroschisis alone.")
para("For counselling, the data support telling families that silo-based closure goes with a longer stay in routine practice and that this probably reflects the infants who need a silo. They also support telling families that sutureless closure may shorten ventilation without changing the stay and may increase umbilical hernias that often close without surgery. The data do not justify one strategy for every infant. Future studies should report outcomes by complexity stratum and deposit individual patient data from registries so that bowel condition can be adjusted at the patient level. Pragmatic trials of sutureless against fascial closure in simple gastroschisis should take ventilator days, umbilical hernia and neurodevelopment as outcomes.")
H('Conclusions')
para("Delayed closure after a silo is associated with longer stay, ventilation and parenteral nutrition than fascial closure. The association is imprecise, heterogeneous and open to confounding by indication, and the trials are too small to settle it. Sutureless closure may shorten ventilation with no clear effect on stay. Certainty is low or very low. Trials or adjusted individual patient analyses that stratify by bowel condition are needed.")
H('Declarations')
para("Funding. «[None declared, authors to confirm.]»")
para("Competing interests. «[None declared, authors to confirm.]»")
para("Ethics. Ethical approval and informed consent were not required because the review used only published data.")
para("Data availability. The extraction workbook, analysis dataset and R scripts are in the Supplementary Material. «[A new archive with a DOI will be created at submission. The earlier archive, 10.5281/zenodo.22989879, belongs to the previous version.]»")
para("Use of AI tools. «[The authors should state here, in the wording the target journal requires, any use of AI tools for data extraction, analysis scripts or drafting, and confirm that all values were checked against the source reports.]»")
D.add_page_break(); H('References')
